    {"problem": "It hummed on the first hot day, then nothing", "solution": "Months of freeze and thaw are hard on a run capacitor, and the first real demand in June is when it lets go. The compressor hums, can't start and trips. We test the capacitor and contactor, replace what failed and check the compressor amperage so you know it survived."},
    {"problem": "The top grille is dented and the fan rattles", "solution": "Ice sliding off a steel or shingle roof lands on whatever is below, and an AC under the eaves takes the hit. A bent fan blade wobbles, chews its motor bearing and can strike the shroud. We replace the blade or motor, straighten crushed fins and talk about a better spot or a stand if the unit is in the slide path."},
    {"problem": "The unit sits tilted on a heaved pad", "solution": "Frost heave lifts one corner of a ground pad every winter. A tilted condenser strains the line set at the service valves, which is where leaks start, and it hurts oil return to the compressor. We relevel or replace the pad, or move the unit to a raised stand, and pressure test the lines."},
    {"problem": "Chewed wires and a nest in the cabinet", "solution": "A warm condenser cabinet is prime winter real estate for mice. They strip the low-voltage wiring and pack the base with nesting that blocks drainage. We clean it out, repair the wiring and seal the obvious entry points."},
    {"problem": "Cool-ish air and frost on the copper line", "solution": "A refrigerant leak, often at a joint stressed by ice load or a heaved pad, or a dirty coil starving the system of airflow. We find the leak with nitrogen and a detector, repair it, pull a vacuum and weigh in a fresh charge rather than topping up every summer."},
    {"problem": "Main floor fine, second floor an oven", "solution": "Painswick and Holly two-storeys with one system and a basement thermostat are the classic case. The problem is airflow and balance, not always the AC. We check the blower speed, dampers and coil before anyone talks about a bigger unit."},
